course: religion-ethics
lesson: 35
title: Virtues: courage, responsibility, rights and duties
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Courage is not having no fear; it is doing what is right even when you are afraid or it is unpopular, like David facing Goliath, or a child who speaks up when everyone else laughs at someone.
explain: Moral courage is everyday courage: standing up for someone, saying no to a dare, or owning up to a mistake; it is hard, but it builds trust and character.
explain: Responsibility means doing your duties and owning the results of your actions instead of making excuses; Christians believe God gives each person gifts and tasks to look after well.
explain: Every child has rights, such as safety, education, being heard and freedom of belief (UN Convention on the Rights of the Child), and rights go with duties: my right is your duty to respect, and your right is mine.
explain: Good rules protect people, though some rules can be unfair and need changing by fair means; self-control and perseverance, managing your wants and keeping going when it is hard, help you do the right thing day after day.
fact: Courage means doing what is right even when it is scary or unpopular.
fact: Courage is not having no fear; it is acting well despite fear.
fact: Moral courage means standing up for what is right, for example speaking up for someone or admitting a mistake.
fact: Responsibility means taking care of your duties and owning the results of your actions.
fact: Owning up means admitting when you have done something wrong; it builds trust.
fact: Rights are things every person is entitled to, such as safety, education, being heard and freedom of belief.
fact: The UN Convention on the Rights of the Child, an agreement made by the countries of the world at the United Nations, lists the rights of every child, whoever they are.
fact: Duties are things we ought to do for others, such as respecting their rights and helping.
fact: Rights and duties go together: one person's right is everyone else's duty to respect.
fact: Rules are agreed guides for behaviour at home, at school and in society.
fact: Good rules protect people; unfair rules can be changed by fair and peaceful means.
fact: Self-control means managing your feelings and wants instead of letting them manage you.
fact: Perseverance means keeping going when things are difficult.
fact: Christians believe God gives people gifts and tasks and asks them to use them well.
exercise: What is courage? | Doing what is right even when it is scary | Never feeling afraid of anything at all, ever, in your whole life | Taking dangerous dares to look brave | Fighting anyone who annoys you
exercise: Does a brave person feel no fear? | No, they act well despite fear | Yes, brave people never feel fear | Yes, fear means you are a coward | No, brave people only pretend
exercise: Which is an example of moral courage? | Speaking up for a child everyone is laughing at | Jumping off a high wall because of a dare | Shouting rudely at a teacher in front of the whole class | Keeping quiet so nobody laughs at you
exercise: What does responsibility mean? | Doing your duties and owning the results of your actions | Making sure someone else gets blamed instead | Doing only the jobs that are fun and easy, and leaving the rest | Following orders without ever thinking
exercise: What does owning up mean? | Admitting you have done something wrong | Saying that something belongs to you and nobody else | Telling on a friend to a teacher to stay out of trouble | Paying for a broken thing secretly so nobody knows
exercise: Why is owning up worth it, even though it is hard? | It builds trust and character | It means you will never be punished | It makes the mistake disappear | It makes others take the blame
exercise: Which is a right of every child? | Education | A new phone | Staying up late | Choosing all the rules
exercise: Which document lists the rights of every child? | The UN Convention on the Rights of the Child | The Ten Commandments in Exodus | The school timetable for the year | The Highway Code for drivers
exercise: What is a duty? | Something we ought to do for others | Something we are owed by other people around us | A prize given for good behaviour at school | A punishment given for bad behaviour at home
exercise: How do rights and duties go together? | My right is your duty to respect, and the other way round | Adults have duties, and children only ever have rights and nothing else | Rights belong to the strong, duties to the weak | They have nothing to do with each other
exercise: Why do we have rules? | Good rules protect people and help us live together | Rules exist only to stop children from having any fun at all | Rules are made so adults can punish more | Rules are just old habits with no purpose
exercise: What should people do about an unfair rule? | Work to change it by fair, peaceful means | Break it in secret whenever they like and hope nobody sees | Obey it for ever, without ever saying a single word | Fight the people who made it until they give in
exercise: What is self-control? | Managing your feelings and wants | Controlling what other people do | Never showing any feelings at all | Doing whatever you feel like
exercise: What is perseverance? | Keeping going when things are difficult | Giving up as soon as it starts to get hard | Doing a job once and never trying it again | Asking others to finish your work for you
exercise: Which Bible story shows courage despite fear? | David facing Goliath | The Garden of Eden | The Tower of Babel | Noah's flood coming to an end
exercise: What do Christians believe about people's gifts? | God gives gifts and tasks to use well | Gifts are only for rich and important people | Gifts must be hidden away from other people | Gifts come only from luck and nothing else
test: "mate dared me to climb the garage roof, I'd look like a chicken if I don't." What is the best-reasoned choice? | Say no; real courage is not taking stupid risks to look cool | Do it, because looking brave matters most | Do it, but only if nobody is filming | Say no, and tell everyone he's a loser
test: Your brother says courage means not being scared of anything. Put him right. | Courage is acting well even while you are scared | He's right, brave people feel no fear | Courage means scaring other people | Courage is only for soldiers, police and firefighters
test: You broke a window playing ball and nobody saw. What is the best-reasoned choice? | Own up and help pay; it is honest and keeps trust | Run off, because nobody saw it so it doesn't really count | Blame the wind, since windows break anyway | Wait and own up only if someone asks
test: An atheist classmate asks: "Do Christians think only they have rights?" Best answer? | No, every person has the same rights, whatever they believe | Yes, rights come only from going to church | Yes, but other people get some rights too | No, Christians think nobody has rights
test: "shut up, I've got freedom of speech, I can say what I like about her." What is the best-reasoned reply? | Your right to speak comes with a duty to respect her rights too | Fine, rights mean you can do anything you want | You don't have any rights, you're a child | Just hit back, that's your right too
test: The class rule says: "Only the boys play football at break." What should the class do? | Ask the teacher to change it fairly, since it shuts girls out | Obey it, because rules are rules | Ignore the rule and start a fight | Make a new rule only girls can play
test: Ava wants to scream at her sister for wrecking her Lego, but counts to ten first. What is she showing? | Self-control | Perseverance | Owning up | Moral courage
test: Noah failed the swimming test twice and goes again the next week. What is he showing? | Perseverance | Self-control | Gratitude | Equity
test: "it's not my fault I forgot, the bag was stupid." What is missing from this excuse? | Responsibility: owning what you did | Courage: being brave on the bus to school | Equity: getting what you need | Gratitude: thanking the bag
test: Judge's follow-up: "Should children always obey every rule?" Best-reasoned answer? | Mostly, as good rules protect us; unfair ones should be changed by fair means | Yes, always, because rules are made by adults | No, rules are for babies and can be ignored | Only when somebody is watching
test: Kai's mum makes him feed the dog every day. Is that fair? | Yes, a pet's care is a duty that comes with having one | No, children should have no duties at all until they grow up | Yes, but only because Kai is the youngest | No, the dog can look after itself
test: Who showed moral courage? | Lily, who told the class it was her pen that leaked | Max, who climbed a tree to show off | Zoe, who stayed quiet while Sam was teased by the others | Finn, who blamed the leak on the new kid
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the virtue of courage? | The performance of right action notwithstanding the presence of fear | The complete absence of fear in all circumstances | The undertaking of hazardous acts for admiration | The readiness to resort to force against others
exam: Identify the principal relationship obtaining between rights and duties. | The right of one person imposes upon others a duty to respect it | Rights pertain exclusively to adults and duties to minors | Rights and duties are wholly unconnected | Duties are owed solely by those devoid of rights
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established approach to an unjust rule. | It ought to be amended by fair and peaceable means | It ought to be obeyed in perpetuity without objection | It ought to be disregarded at the discretion of each person | It ought to be resisted by means of force
exam: Ascertain which of the following constitutes an example of owning up. | A pupil admitting to a teacher that he broke a ruler | A pupil reporting to the teacher that another pupil broke a ruler | A pupil concealing the pieces of a broken ruler | A pupil purchasing a new ruler in secret
